<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<section xml:id="section-ht-single-proportion" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Section Name</title>
  <p> 
    In <xref ref="chapter-hypothesis-setup"/>, I spent many sections
    building up the ideas and definitions of hypothesis testing. In
    those sections, we primarily focused on a difference of
    proportions. There is good reason for this: a controlled trial is
    always a difference in proportions and a controlled trial is one
    of the most fundamental ways to design a research study. But there
    are other types of statistics. We are now moving on to just a
    single proportion instead of a difference between two proportions.
  </p>
  <p>
    I've introduced the different kinds of sample statistic before,
    but let me review this particular one. What kind of statistic is a
    single proportion? Well, it is a proportion, a percentage, but
    only one. We are asking about the value of a single percentage
    observation. It is still a categorical variable with two options.
    It's like a trial but without a control group: there is only the
    treatment group. 
  </p>
  <p>
    There is still a proportion: the cases still falling into one of
    two categories in some categorical variable. Generically, I still
    call these categories <sq>successful</sq> and
    <sq>unsuccessful</sq>, though what that actually means depends a
    great deal on the particular study. Many studies will have two
    equally appealing outcomes, but we stil need a generic languag, so
    we talk about success for this generic language. 
  </p>
  <p>
    Here are some examples of research questions that lead to a single
    proportion statistic. 
    <ul>
      <li>
        How many canadians suppor the green party? There is only one
        group: Canadians. There are two categories: support or do not
        support. There is one percentage: what percentage support? As
        phrased this way, <sq>success</sq> would be supporting the
        green party, regardless of whether the researchers individual
        political opinions actually favour green party policy. 
      </li>
      <li>
        How often do we have snow in Septmeber in Edmonton? There is
        one categories: years. There are two outcomes: snow or now
        snow. There is one percentage: how many years have snow
        sometime in September. A success is recording snow in
        September, again regardless of whether or not we think snow in
        September is a pleasant thing. 
      </li>
      <li>
        How many restaurants in Edmonton have gluten-free options
        clearly available on their menus? There is one group:
        restaurants in Edmonton. There are two categories: glute free
        options clearly available, or not clearly available. There is
        one percetange: how many restaurants have this? A success is a
        restaurant which has such options.
      </li>
    </ul>
    Hopefully its clear from these examples that many reasonable
    research questions result in a single proportion statistic. 
  </p>
  <p>
    What would a hypothesis be for such a statistic. With difference in
    proportions, there was a comparison of groups. The null hypothesis
    was no difference between the group. Here, there is no second
    group for comparsion, so a null hypothesis of zero makes no sense.
    Instead, the null and alternative hypotheses are always about the
    sample statistic meeting some threshold. Let me go through the
    previous three examples again to give an idea of what this might
    look like. 
    <ul>
      <li>
        If previous polls showed <m>4.3\%</m> support for the green
        party, the researchers might want to know if this support has
        increased. A null hypothesis would be no increas, so <m>x \leq
        0.043</m>. The alternative hypotheses to match would be that
        support has increased: <m>x \gt 0.045</m>.
      </li>
      <li>
        A climate change scientist might wonder if it snows less often
        in September than before. If <m>27\%</m> of Septembers over a
        particular historical span had seen snow, then the null
        hypothesis would that the years in the dataset are just a
        likely to have snow: <m>x \geq 0.027</m>. The alternative
        hypothesis to match would be that the rate of snow has
        actually decreased in recent years: <m>x \lt 0.027</m>. 
      </li>
      <li>
        Maybe the city of Edmonton has a goal of having gluten free
        options present in at least <m>50\%</m> of restaurants and the
        researchers are wondering if the city has hit that goal. The
        null hypothesis would be that they have not hit that goal: <m>
        x \leq 0.05</m>, and the alternative hypothesis would be that
        they have exceeded it: <m>x \gt 0.05</m>. 
      </li>
    </ul> 
    In each case, there is some externally determined threshold that
    the researchers care about: a historical level, a target or goal,
    a safety threshold, some kind of set proportion that is important
    for some reasons. 
  </p>
  <p>
    Let me move on to a specific example which I will use to develop
    the theory in this section. A survey of payday loan borrowers
    asked <m>826</m> people is they supported better regulation on
    payday loan lenders. <m>578</m> responded yes, which is
    <m>70\%</m>. Our sample statistics is <m>70\%</m> or <m>0.70</m>.
    As discussed before, the researchquestion for a single proportion
    is always a threshold question. Here, say we want to know if the
    population parameter (that is, the percentage of all possible
    payday loan borrowers who would answer yes) is at least 65%.
    That's a threshold. We can do hypothesis testing or build a
    confidence interval to understand this threshold and answer the
    question with some standard of confidence.
  </p>
  <p>
    The threshold question gives a hypothesis: is the population
    parameter above <m>65\%</m>?  The null hypothesis answers no: the
    population parameter is <m>65\%</m> of less. The alternative
    hypothesis says that the population parameter is indeed above
    <m>65\%</m>. 
  </p>
  <p>
    So we have our hypothesis question and our sample statistics. The
    quesiton is the same as before: how unlikely is our sample
    statistics to be a random outcome of the null hypothesis? If
    likely, then it is not significant and we do not have evidence for
    the alternative hypothesis. If unlikely, then we is significant,
    we reject the null hypothesis and accept the alternative. 

The setup for this is the same as before: we do randomizations and
study probability to figure this out. I'll leave the randomization
methods aside for now, but we still get a sampling distribution. Under
reasonable conditions, the central limit theorem applies and the
sampling distribution will be normal. The mean of the sampling
distribution will be given by the null hypothesis: in this case, a
mean of 0.65. Our sample statistics is 0.7. We want to know how far
out this is in the sampling distribution: now unlikely this is. 

SLIDE

So we need, again, to understand the sampling distribution. We already
have the mean, since that is given by the null hypothesis. We need the
standard deviation in order to figure out how many standard deviations
away we find our sample statistic, so we can use z-scores and
percentiles to calculate its probability. 

SLIDE

For a difference in proprtions, this was the formula for standard
error, the standard deviation of the sampling distribution. This has
two proprotions and two sample sizes. However, now we only have one
group. Happily for us, having one group just simplifies the standard
error. The formula is just one half of this express, with only one
porportion to worry about. We don't have any pooled proportion
question anymore either, since there aren't two groups to pool
together. 

SLIDE

Let me put the whole calculation together. I have a sample statistics:
0.70. I have a null hypothesis which stipulates a mean of 0.65. I have
a standard error calculated by this square root expression, which has
the null hypothesis proportion and the sample size. I then have the
z-score of the sample statistics, which tells me that is is 2.94
standard deviations away from the mean. I can turn that into a
percentile, which is 0.998 or 99.8th percentile. I can make a p-value
by taking the difference from one, which gives me the probability that
0.7 (or above) was randomly produced by the null hypothesis. This
probability is 0.002, must less that 0.05, so the finding is
signficant. I reject the null hypothesis and conlude that, by the
evidence of this study, the population parameter is indeed above 65%. 

Let me make the same note I did before. In the standard error, the
sample size is in the denominator. That means that larger sample sizes
mean smaller standard error, which mean larger z score, and
percentiles closer to 1, and smaller p-value, and, finally, more
likely to find significant. Larger sample sizes are more likely to
produce significant results, mathematically, according to the
probability theory. 

SLIDE

That's hypothesis testing. I can do the same thing for confidence
intervals. The setup is very similar to what we did before, but let me
go over it. Under reasonable conditions, I can assume the sampling
distribution is normal as before. However, the sampling distributino
for confidence intervals is difference. I don't have a null hypothesis
anymore, I just have the sample statistic: 0.70. That is the mean of
the sample distribution here. The standard error is the same formula,
but now I am using the sample statistic as the mean, not another mean
from a null hypothesis. Here, the standard error works out to 0.016.
That's the standard deviation of this normal sampling distribution. 

Then the range of the 95% confidence interval was given by this
formula, which is still the same. I go out 1.96 standard deviations
from the mean on either side. The numbers here work out to 0.669 to
0.731. I am 95% confidence that the population parameter is between
66.9& and 73.1%. This also matches the hypothesis test: I am 95%
certain that the population parameter is above 0.65, since I am 95%
certain that is lies in this range which does not include 0.65. 

The number 1.96 is for the 95% confidence intervals. Other confidence
intervals have other coefficients here, which I mentioned before. To
change your standard of confidence, you just replace this 1.96 with
the appropriate coefficient. 



\begin{frame}
  \frametitle{Single Proportions}
  \begin{itemize}
    \item[] 
      One categorical variable.
      \vspace{0.5cm}
    \item[] 
      Two categories. Interpreted as one success and one failure. 
      \vspace{0.5cm}
    \item[] 
      How many Canadians support the green party?
      \vspace{0.5cm}
    \item[] 
      How often do we have snow in September in Edmonton?
      \vspace{0.5cm}
    \item[] 
      How many restaurants in Edmonton have gluten-free options
      clearly available on their menus?
  \end{itemize}
\end{frame}

\begin{frame}
  \frametitle{An Example}
  \begin{itemize}
    \item[] 
      Survey of payday loan borrowers. 
      \vspace{0.5cm}
    \item[] 
      Question: do you support better regulation on payday loan
      lenders?
      \vspace{0.5cm}
    \item[] 
      826 Cases. Sample statistics: 70\% of these (578) answers
      ``yes''. 
      \vspace{0.5cm}
    \item[] 
      Question: is the population parameter greater than 65\%?
  \end{itemize}
\end{frame}

\begin{frame}
  \frametitle{Hypothesis Test}
  \begin{itemize}
    \item[] 
      Sample statistics: $\hat{p}= 0.70$
      \vspace{0.5cm}
    \item[] 
      Null hypothesis: $p = 0.65$
      \vspace{0.5cm}
    \item[] 
      Alternative hypothesis: $p > 0.65$
  \end{itemize}
\end{frame}

\begin{frame}
  \frametitle{Sampling Distribution}
  \centering
  \begin{tikzpicture}
    \begin{axis}[
        ytick=\empty,
      ]
      \addplot [
        domain=0.58:0.72,
        samples=200,
      ]
      {exp(-(x-0.65)^2 / (2*(0.017)^2)) / (sqrt(2*pi*(0.017)^2))};
    \end{axis}
  \end{tikzpicture}
\end{frame}

\begin{frame}
  \frametitle{Sampling Distribution}
  \centering
  \begin{tikzpicture}
    \begin{axis}[
        ytick=\empty,
      ]
      \addplot [
        domain=0.58:0.72,
        samples=200,
      ]
      {exp(-(x-0.65)^2 / (2*(0.017)^2)) / (sqrt(2*pi*(0.017)^2))};
    \end{axis}
  \end{tikzpicture}
  \begin{equation*}
    \mu = 0.65 \hspace{2cm} \sigma = SE = ?
  \end{equation*}
\end{frame}

\begin{frame}
  \frametitle{Standard Error}
  \begin{displaymath}
    \begin{array}{rcl}
      SE & = & \sqrt{ \frac{p_1(1-p_1}{n_1} + \frac{p_2(1-p_2)}{n_2}
      }\\[2em]
      SE & = & \sqrt{ \frac{p(1-p)}{n}} 
    \end{array}
  \end{displaymath} 
\end{frame}

\begin{frame}
  \frametitle{Calculating p-value}
  \begin{displaymath}
    \begin{array}{rcl}
      x & = & 0.70 \\[2em]
      \mu & = & 0.65\\[2em]
      SE & = & \frac{(0.65)(1-0.65)}{826} = 0.017\\[2em]
      z(0.70) & = & 2.94 \\[2em]
      \text{percentile} & = & 0.998 \\[2em]
      p & = & 1 - 0.992 = 0.002 < 0.05 
    \end{array}
  \end{displaymath} 
\end{frame}

\begin{frame}
  \frametitle{Calculating Confidence Intervals}
  \begin{displaymath}
    \begin{array}{rcl}
      x & = & 0.70 \\[2em]
      \mu & = & 0.75\\[2em]
      SE & = & \frac{(0.75)(1-0.75)}{826} = 0.016\\[2em]
      \text{95\% Interval} &  = & [\mu - 1.96(SE), \mu + 1.96(SE)] \\[2em]
      \text{95\% Interval} &  = & [0.669,0.731] \\[2em]
    \end{array}
  \end{displaymath} 
\end{frame}

\end{document}
